# FlyRank AI Internship — Final Capstone Project

## Project Title
"Which Pages Should You Refresh First?" — A Ranking Model for Content 
Opportunity Scoring

## Overview
This is the final, mandatory capstone deliverable of my FlyRank AI Machine 
Learning Engineering internship (ML-CAP-01). It consolidates all 8 weeks of 
assignment work into one complete, deployed research paper, built on real 
FlyRank search data.

## What This Project Does
Content editorial teams cannot manually review every page in a large content 
library. This project builds a ranking model that answers a concrete 
decision: which content pages should an editor review first for a refresh, 
given limited time?

## Lane Chosen
Refresh / Content Opportunity Scoring — one of four predefined Search 
Intelligence lanes offered in the program.

## What I Built
1. **Data contract** — verified against FlyRank's real production data 
   warehouse (~79 million rows, queried directly via DuckDB).
2. **Transparent baseline rule** — a simple, human-readable rule (stale + 
   still-visible pages), scoring Precision@50 = 0.240.
3. **Machine learning model** — a Random Forest ranking model, honestly 
   validated on a client-holdout split (never a random split, to avoid the 
   model memorizing client-specific patterns), reaching Precision@50 = 0.540 
   — a measured ~2.25x improvement over the baseline.
4. **Full leakage audit** — including a deliberate leak test that confirmed 
   the validation process correctly detects leakage when present.
5. **Ranked action playbook** — the model's output packaged into a 
   human-reviewed queue with reason codes, explicit human sign-off rules, 
   and a "do-not-automate" list.
6. **Deployed research paper** — a complete public research paper covering 
   Abstract, Introduction, Data, Methodology, Results (with charts), 
   Limitations, Ranked Recommendations, Reproducibility, and Acknowledgments 
   & data credit (linked to flyrank.ai).

## What I Learned
- How to take a real business question and correctly frame it as a specific 
  ML task (ranking/scoring), rather than jumping straight to modeling.
- Why validation design (grouped/client-holdout splits) matters more than 
  the model itself — an honest, slightly lower score is worth more than an 
  inflated, leaked one.
- How to turn a technical result into a publicly shareable, honestly-worded 
  research artifact that a stranger can trust in minutes.

## Challenges Faced
- Working entirely from a mobile device for an 8-week technical internship, 
  including Colab notebooks, GitHub commits, and deploying a live webpage.
- Debugging data warehouse queries against real column names and correctly 
  configuring authentication for a gated, production-scale dataset.
- Condensing eight weeks of technical work into a concise, honest, 
  non-overclaiming research paper.

